[Music]  the ultimate goal of the european union  is to replace all animals used in  science  much progress has been made but many of  the solutions that make us live better  and longer  still continue to involve animals we  must all work together  to move away from using animals in  science and testing  an effective way of advancing this is to  better understand  where when how and which animals are  used in europe  allures is the eu's new database on the  use of animals for scientific purposes  it's the most comprehensive repository  on animal use  for scientific purposes in the world  the user-friendly allures is free and  accessible to everyone  whether you're an interested citizen an  ngo a scientist  or a policy maker there is more  information available to you today  than ever before get data on each use of  an animal  and what type of procedures are  performed learn in which areas  animals are still needed for research  and testing and what types of animals  are being used  genetically altered animals are animals  whose genes have been  either genetically engineered to  incorporate a gene  or to inactivate a gene or it can be  nature  itself that have changed a gene so the  benefits  in scientific research of using  genetically altered animals  is that it offers the means of new and  better ways of  understanding preventing and treating  human diseases find out which areas use  the most  animals in which procedures animals can  experience the most suffering  and which legal requirements lead to  animal use  identify where science urgently needs to  develop alternative approaches  determine where to focus r d efforts and  funding  the eu legislation requires that we  minimise any pain or distress to animals  and we can do this in a whole range of  ways  for example we can use anaesthetics we  can use pain relieving drugs  we can design studies so that they cause  the minimum of pain and distress to  animals  and we can of course house them in the  best possible way  so the eu legislation requires that the  severity of procedures  on animals must be reported but what do  we mean by severity  let me give you an example if an animal  receives a new medicine  and has a blood sample taken that would  be a mild level of severity  if that new medicine caused some side  effects  then that might become a moderate  procedure  and if those side effects became really  major  and for an exact for example an animal  died then that would be a severe  procedure  the eu is committed to the protection  and welfare of animals  still used in science all animal  research and testing in europe  must comply with very strict laws no  living animal can be used for scientific  purposes  without prior authorization as soon as  an  alternative non-animal method is  available animal use can no longer be  authorized  the eu legislation protects animals used  for science  in europe by setting out a framework  and minimum standards for regulating the  use of animals in science  this legislation makes the use of  animals in science more transparent  because researchers and member states  must report  how many animals they use each year as  well as the species involved  and the purposes for which these animals  are used  this transparency helps to protect  animals  because we are able to better understand  where more focus needs to be directed  towards the promotion  of animal-free approaches we need this  transparency  because the public have many concerns  around these use of animals they expect  every possible action to be taken to  ultimately end the use of animals  and their suffering let's work together  to care for animals and aim for better  science

